+# Decision: Preview cache replacement policy
+
+## Context
+
+The preview worker keeps a private, bounded cache of rendered contact sheets: at most 12 GiB of cached JPEG data, serving roughly six thousand distinct clips a week to twenty-four editors. Two ways of working pull against each other inside that budget. A screening walks a long, mostly sequential run of clips, touching each sheet once and never returning to it. A comparison session returns to the same small set of sheets again and again, sometimes across an hour of screening. The cache is shared by all editors, so one editor's screening competes with another editor's comparison set for the same bytes.
+
+The replacement policy decides what the cache keeps when it must make room. Getting it wrong costs re-renders at roughly 450 ms per sheet, but the cost lands unevenly: a policy that flushes the working set penalises the editor who is comparing footage, while a policy that holds on to everything popular eventually starves the sheets an editor is about to need.
+
+Three properties are inherited at this node and are not choices here. The cached data stays within the 12 GiB bound. The preview interface — 200 with a JPEG, 202 with a job ticket and a two-second retry interval, 404 for an unknown revision — and the four-way render queue concurrency do not change. All replacement bookkeeping stays inside the worker, so no other node reads queue state, hits, or pins entries. All three options below respect these properties, so they eliminate none of them.
+
+## Decision Elements
+
+**Gates — inherited constraints**
+
+- **G1 — Bounded cache.** The policy must keep cached JPEG data within 12 GiB, evicting before admission when the bound would be exceeded.
+- **G2 — Fixed external behavior.** The policy must not alter the preview interface or the render queue's concurrency.
+- **G3 — Private bookkeeping.** The policy's queues, counters, and key lists must remain inside the worker.
+
+**Weighted criteria**
+
+- **C1 — Protection of reused sheets during a screening (major).** The sheets an editor returns to within a session should survive a concurrent sequential scan of one-use sheets.
+- **C2 — Isolation between editors (moderate).** A screening run by one editor should not flush another editor's working set, since the cache is shared.
+- **C3 — Adaptation after a workload shift (moderate).** After a project switch, the cache should stop favouring yesterday's sheets without operator intervention.
+- **C4 — Bookkeeping cost per request (moderate).** The work the policy adds to the request path — counter updates, queue moves, key lookups.
+- **C5 — Simplicity of the resulting structure (minor).** How many queues and tunable parameters a maintainer must reason about.
+- **C6 — Metadata footprint (minor).** Memory and I/O spent on bookkeeping rather than on preview bytes.
+
+## Options in the Domain
+
+### Least recently used (LRU)
+
+**Description.** A single ordered set of cached sheets. Every request moves the sheet it hits to the most-recently-used end; every admission puts the new sheet there too. When the 12 GiB bound requires space, the sheet at the least-recently-used end is evicted. No counters, no second queue, no key list — the order of the set is the whole policy.
+
+**Analysis.**
+
+- G1 — passes: a single ordered set is bounded by the same admission check as any other policy.
+- G2 — passes: replacement is entirely internal to the worker.
+- G3 — passes: one order over cached keys.
+- C1 — weak: the sheets a screening touches are, by construction, the most recently used ones, so the comparison set is evicted exactly when the scan fills the cache.
+- C2 — weak: the same reasoning holds across editors, because one editor's scan is also the most recent use.
+- C3 — strong: the set always reflects present work, so a project switch is followed automatically.
+- C4 — strong: one position update per request.
+- C5 — strong: one structure and no tunable parameters.
+- C6 — strong: one order entry per cached sheet.
+
+**Summary.** The cheapest policy, and the one that follows current work exactly — including a scan.
+
+### Frequency-based eviction with aging
+
+**Description.** Each cached sheet carries a hit count. When the cache must make room, the sheets whose value is lowest are evicted, where value is a count that decays over time: an access refreshes a sheet's count, and a periodic pass ages the counts so that old popularity fades. The result approximates "the sheets people keep coming back to", and the aging keeps that answer from freezing on one period's favourites.
+
+**Analysis.**
+
+- G1 — passes: eviction is driven by the same admission check.
+- G2 — passes: counts are internal state.
+- G3 — passes: counts never leave the worker.
+- C1 — partial: a comparison set accumulates more hits than a screening's one-use sheets, so it tends to survive; but counts saturate, and a sheet seen once in an earlier session can outrank a sheet used heavily today.
+- C2 — partial: popularity is measured in aggregate across editors, so one editor's brief repeated access can outrank another editor's working set.
+- C3 — weak: aging is deliberately slow, so yesterday's favourites outlive the project that produced them; ageing fast enough to fix that approaches plain LRU and gives up C1 with it.
+- C4 — weak: every request touches a counter, and aging requires periodic maintenance across all keys.
+- C5 — moderate: one structure, but a counter rule and an aging schedule to reason about.
+- C6 — moderate: a counter per cached sheet plus the aging bookkeeping.
+
+**Summary.** Protects what is provably popular, at the price of counters and a slow memory of popularity.
+
+### 2Q
+
+**Description.** Two queues of cached keys, plus a list of evicted keys. A newly rendered sheet enters the cold queue, which holds sheets requested once. A second request promotes that sheet to the hot queue, which holds the sheets an editor keeps returning to. Eviction drains the cold queue first, taking the sheet held there longest, and only then takes the least recently used hot sheet. A ghost list of evicted cold keys recognizes a re-request of a sheet that was evicted, so the sheet returns to the hot queue instead of starting over in the cold queue. The hot queue is bounded by its own share of the cache budget — currently starting at one quarter of the 12 GiB bound — and when it exceeds that share its least recently used sheet is demoted to the cold queue rather than deleted.
+
+**Analysis.**
+
+- G1 — passes: the hot share is a fraction of the same 12 GiB budget, and both queues are bounded by it.
+- G2 — passes: all state is worker-private and nothing about the policy reaches the interface.
+- G3 — passes: queue membership and the ghost list never leave the worker.
+- C1 — strong: one-use scan sheets sit in the cold queue and are evicted first, while the comparison set sits in the hot queue and survives the scan.
+- C2 — strong: protection follows a key's own reuse rather than aggregate popularity, so another editor's scan fills the cold queue and leaves comparison sets resident.
+- C3 — moderate: the hot queue is bounded and demotes its least recently used sheet, so old hot sheets leave on their own; the purge is slower than LRU's but bounded, and the hot share can be retuned from pilot measurements.
+- C4 — moderate: a request updates a queue position and may move the sheet between queues, and a miss that follows an eviction costs a ghost-list lookup.
+- C5 — weak: two queues, a ghost list, and a tunable hot share.
+- C6 — partial: queue entries for cached sheets plus a bounded key list, and no per-key counters.
+
+**Summary.** Separates "seen once" from "reused" — the distinction the workload actually makes — at the cost of two queues and a tuning parameter.
+
+## Selection
+
+2Q is chosen. It is the only option strong on C1, the major criterion: the screening/comparison split is a distinction between one-use and reused sheets, and 2Q is the only policy that represents that distinction directly rather than inferring it from recency or from accumulated counts. It is also strong on C2, which the other two fail in different ways — LRU flushes other editors' work by recency, frequency by aggregate popularity.
+
+Against C3, LRU is the stronger option and 2Q is only moderate: adaptation is bounded rather than immediate. That cost is accepted, because the comparison set is worth more than instant forgetting, and because a bounded hot queue with demotion still sheds old sheets without operator intervention. Against C4 and C5, 2Q is the most expensive option on both counts, and that price is accepted as the direct cost of the protection in C1 — the bookkeeping is the mechanism, not overhead to be minimised away.
+
+Committing to 2Q forecloses per-clip pinning, a "protect this sheet" request, per-editor cache quotas, and any policy that adapts without operator tuning — each would cross G3 or replace the two-queue structure. It obliges the team to size the hot queue's share of the budget and the ghost list's key capacity, and to revisit this decision if pilot measurements show comparison sets small enough that C3 outweighs C1.
+
+The decision turns on the tenet that a cache miss is ordinary behaviour rather than an API failure: eviction and re-render are normal outcomes, so the policy is free to optimise for which sheets stay resident rather than to defend against the miss. It also respects the tenet that cache paths and replacement bookkeeping stay private to the preview worker, as gate G3.
